const faq = [
  { q: 'Is seeing a raven a sign of death?', a: 'In Western folk tradition (British, Irish, continental European), a raven near a home, following a person, or calling persistently was interpreted as a death omen. This tradition is culturally specific — the same raven behaviour is interpreted positively in Norse tradition (Odin\'s attention) and Pacific Northwest Indigenous tradition (Raven as a creator figure). Whether a raven is a sign of death depends entirely on which cultural tradition you apply; there is no universal biological or metaphysical basis for the belief.' },
  { q: 'Why are ravens associated with death?', a: 'Ravens are associated with death for a concrete biological reason: they are scavengers that reliably appear at large animal corpses, including human battle dead and execution sites. This real-world presence at human death scenes created the association in multiple independent traditions across Eurasia. The raven\'s black plumage, loud call, and intelligent opportunism made it memorable and frightening in this context. The association is not arbitrary superstition but a direct reflection of the raven\'s ecological role as a carrion consumer that gathers where death has occurred.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens good luck in any culture?', a: 'Yes — in several traditions, ravens are strongly positive symbols. In Norse tradition, ravens are Odin\'s birds — divine messengers and instruments of wisdom; encountering a raven was potentially a sign of the god\'s attention. In Pacific Northwest Indigenous traditions, Raven is the Trickster-Creator who stole light for humanity — a beneficent, if ambiguous, figure. In the Tower of London tradition, ravens protect the kingdom — their presence is essential to Britain\'s safety. In many Indigenous North American and Siberian traditions, ravens are ancestor spirits, clan totems, and helpers. The "bad luck" reading is a specifically Western European folk tradition, not a universal.'
  },
];
